.TH CBMAP_REDECLARE 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cbmap_redeclare \- re-establish the companion type variables for an ordered map handle from another scope
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cbstmap.h>

cbmap_redeclare(hm_name, key_t, val_t);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Declares the companion type variables that carry key_t and val_t for an
ordered map handle that already exists. It declares no handle, and it
neither creates nor destroys a map; all it gives back is the type
information that the type-inferred macros need.

Each type-inferred macro learns the key type and the value type of a map
from two ordinary local variables named after the handle, which
cbmap_construct(3) and cbmap_declare(3) create next to it. Being local
declarations, they don't travel with the handle into another function.
Inside that function cbmap_insert(hm_name, ...) and its siblings would
name variables that nothing declares there, and the translation unit
would not compile. This macro declares those variables again, so every
caller that passes a cbmap across a function boundary needs it.

The types must be the ones the caller created the map with, because the
macros use them to read a key, to size a value and to decide whether a
value is a char * that the map stores by contents.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
hm_name  Name of the map handle that already exists
key_t    Key type that the caller created the map with
val_t    Value type that the caller created the map with
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
None. The macro expands to declarations, not to an expression.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Because the macro is a sequence of declarations, put it where a
declaration is legal and end it with a semicolon of its own.

The declared variables are pointers that nothing dereferences at run time.
They are there so that typeof and the type-introspection macros can read
the types from them, and they cost nothing in the generated code.

Since it only declares variables, this macro is also how you reach a
handle held in a struct field or a global: name it in each function that
works on that handle.

An array key type or value type, such as int[2] or char[16], is a compile
error whose message says to wrap the array in a struct. The macros copy
keys and values by assignment and C can't assign an array, so the map
would otherwise store the address of the caller's array instead of its
elements. A struct that holds the array works.
.fi
.SH WARNINGS
.nf
The declared value type can have a different width from the stored one.
cbmap_get(3) and cbmap_get_ptr(3) catch that at run time: they describe
the size mismatch and stop the program through _ccol_fatal_err(). They
can't see a mismatch between two types of the same width, though, so the
types given here are a promise that the caller keeps and the compiler
doesn't check. A key of the wrong width is reported rather than accepted,
since the raw entry points return ccol_invalid_args for it. The order of
the tree only means something for keys of the width that the caller
created the map for.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
void bump(cbmap scores, int who) {
    cbmap_redeclare(scores, int, int);
    int *slot = cbmap_get_ptr(scores, who);
    if (slot) {
        (*slot)++;
    }
}

cbmap_construct(scores, int, int);
int who = 7, score = 42;
cbmap_insert(scores, who, score);
bump(scores, 7);
cbmap_destroy(scores);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cbmap_declare (3),
.BR cbmap_construct (3),
.BR cbmap_get (3),
.BR cbmap_get_ptr (3),
.BR cbmap_insert (3)
